※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ The startup function takes the record the process was started with, not a reference to it.
※
※ There is one of it -- `std.Init` is `@[unique]` -- so having it is having the thing itself,
※ and a program that passes it on takes the reference where it passes it.  `mut` before the
※ type says the program may write the record it was handed.
※
※ What the entry point hands over is still *where* the record is: it is in the image, the
※ entry point filled it, and there is no caller to put it in registers.  That is what a record
※ name already means everywhere else -- a record is somewhere and a field of it is read at an
※ offset from where -- so the signature a program writes says the record and nothing is copied.

let std := ⎕import("std")

@[impure]
fn said(init: &std.Init) → u64 ¤size:
    #init⌖.args

@[startup, impure]
fn main(init: mut std.Init) → u6:
    ※ A field of the record, read at an offset from where it is.  How many words the
    ※ program was named with depends on how it was run, so what is checked is that the
    ※ two ways of asking agree -- and that there is at least the name itself.
    let count: u64 = ⎕drop(#init.args)
    if count = 0u64:
        return 1u6
    ※ Passed along by reference, which is how a value that is never copied travels.
    if ⎕drop(said(&init)) ≠ count:
        return 2u6
    ※ The devices, read through the record.
    if init.io.output.fd ≠ 1i32:
        return 3u6
    ※ And written, which is what `mut` says may be done.
    init.io.output ← std.Writer(.fd ← 2i32)
    if init.io.output.fd ≠ 2i32:
        return 4u6
    0u6
